INT. HOSPITAL ROOM 412 - EARLY MORNING

Blinds half-open. A low monitor beeps. A tray with an untouched breakfast. A vase of wilting carnations on the sill.

DR. HYE-WON PARK, forties, a gray cardigan over her scrubs, enters with a chart. MR. LINDGREN, seventy, lies propped in bed in a thin hospital gown, white stubble, an IV in his hand. NURSE ABIOLA, thirties, adjusting a pillow, glances up.

HYE-WON
Good morning, Mr. Lindgren.

LINDGREN
Is it?

HYE-WON
I hope so.

She sets the chart down. Pulls a stool to the bedside. Abiola steps back, letting her take the space.

HYE-WON (CONT'D)
How did you sleep?

LINDGREN
Fine.

ABIOLA
He was up twice.

LINDGREN
I was not.

ABIOLA
You asked me for the radio.

LINDGREN
That's not sleeping badly. That's listening.

Hye-won almost smiles. She reaches for his wrist, checks his pulse.

HYE-WON
Your tests came back.

LINDGREN
And?

HYE-WON
I'd like to go through them with you.

LINDGREN
Just say it.

Hye-won exchanges a glance with Abiola. Abiola looks at the floor.

HYE-WON
It's in the pancreas. It has spread to the liver.

He turns his head to the window. His jaw works. The monitor keeps its steady beep.

LINDGREN
How long?

HYE-WON
Months. Perhaps less.

He nods. Reaches for the glass of water on the tray. His hand shakes. Abiola moves to help but he waves her off. He drinks. Sets the glass down with great care.

LINDGREN
My wife used to say I'd outlive the doctors.

HYE-WON
She might have been right.

LINDGREN
She was usually right. It was annoying.

Abiola laughs, a short sound, then covers her mouth. Hye-won puts a hand on his arm.

HYE-WON
We'll keep you comfortable.

LINDGREN
I know.

He picks at the corner of the blanket. Abiola straightens the carnations, plucks off a brown petal.

LINDGREN (CONT'D)
Can I have the radio?

ABIOLA
Of course.

She fetches it from the drawer, plugs it in, and finds a station. A soft piano. He closes his eyes.
